# K18 guard corpus — THE ARM-ORDER AXIS (docs/dev/known_issues.md K18, FIXED
# 2026-08-15; docs/design/k18_memo_design.md §1.5 and §5 item 1).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and it is the more useful half of the K18 landing.
# The 165-case acceptance corpus beside this one was derived from K18 as
# FOUND: every diverging pattern in it carries a LAZY nullable arm, because
# the witness did. R23's semantics critic showed that laziness is not the
# ingredient. What the defect needs is that the arm whose EXIT edge lands on
# the already-seen epsilon state is the PREFERRED one — and a GREEDY nullable
# arm achieves exactly that by being written FIRST. Two of the K18 entry's own
# parenthesised "does NOT diverge" causes were therefore wrong, and their
# mirror images were live tier-1 miscompiles on the shipped compiler:
#
#     (?:(?:b*|a)?)*        "ba"   was [0,2), both oracles [0,1)
#     (?:(?:b?|a)?)*        "ba"   was [0,2), both oracles [0,1)
#     (?:(?:(?:b|)|a)?)*    "ba"   was [0,2), both oracles [0,1)   (no quantifier at all)
#     (?:(?:b?|a)(?:b?|d))* "ba"   was [0,2), both oracles [0,1)   (concatenation)
#
# So this file crosses every diverging shape with BOTH alternation orders and
# with greedy as well as lazy nullable arms, plus the empty-alternative and
# concatenation forms that contain no lazy quantifier anywhere. The last nine
# blocks are over-reach controls — including K17's own repro, a pattern with
# no nullable loop at all (the closure's empty-context fast path), and the
# memo-pricing family `(?:a*|b*){3}`.
#
# A control that is one character away from a witness is not an over-reach
# control. That is the lesson this axis encodes.
#
# Every expectation generated from python3 `re` AND libpcre2 10.46, both
# oracles agreeing on every cell (D44's three-way rule).

pattern (?:(?:a|b*?)?)*
